#### case 8 diagnose
#### When a program goes wrong, the diagnosis names the cause in the program's terms.
effect Ask { ask(Int) }  fn main() / {Ask} { ask(1) }
#### expect
operation `ask` is performed, but no handler for it is installed
#### case 9 diagnose
fn f(x: Int) -> Int { match(x) { 0 => 1 } }  fn main() -> Int { f(5) }
#### expect
no clause of a callable in `f` matches the arguments (5)
#### case 10 diagnose
fn main() -> Int { match(Some(1)) { None => 0 } }
#### expect
no clause of a callable in `main` matches the arguments (Ok(1))
#### case 11 diagnose
effect Ask { ask() }  fn main() -> Int { handle ask() { ask(k) => k(1) + k(2) } }
#### expect
a resumption is used a second time: it was already resumed or cancelled
#### case 12 diagnose
fn main() -> Int { let l = @out { @out }; l(1) }
#### expect
a label is applied after its scope has ended
#### case 13 diagnose
fn main() -> Int { let x = 5; x(1) }
#### expect
`5` is called, but it is not callable
#### case 14 diagnose
fn main() -> Int { 9223372036854775807 + 1 }
#### expect
integer overflow: the result of an operation on (9223372036854775807, 1) does not fit in 64 bits
#### case 15 diagnose
fn main() -> Int { (1, 2).5 }
#### expect
`(1, 2)` does not have the field being read (in `main`)
#### case 16 diagnose
fn f<T, U>(a: T, b: U) -> T { a }  fn main() -> Int { f(1) }
#### expect
no clause of `f` matches the arguments (1)
#### case 17 diagnose
fn main() -> Int { 1 + 2 }
#### expect
did not go wrong
#### case 18 diagnose
#### A program the checker rejects is elaborated without types; its blocks still
#### take the arguments the functions they are passed to give them.
effect Ask { ask() -> Int }
    fn main() -> Int { handle ask() + 1 { ask(k) => (if(2 < 1) { k(1) } else { 0 }) + k(2) + k(3) } }
#### expect
a resumption is used a second time: it was already resumed or cancelled
#### case 19 liveCells
#### A mutable variable's cell is released however its block is left: by
#### finishing, by a label, by a terminal operation, or by cancellation.
fn bump(n: Int) -> Int { let mut x = n; x = x + 1; x }
    fn main() -> Int { let mut i = 0; while({ i < 20 }) { i = i + bump(0) }; i }
#### expect
some 0
#### case 20 liveCells
fn main() -> Int { @out { let mut x = 1; @out(x); 0 } }
#### expect
some 0
#### case 21 liveCells
effect Fail { fail(Int) -> ! }  fn work() -> Int / {Fail} { let mut x = 7; fail(x); 0 }
    fn main() -> Int { handle work() { fail(e) => e } }
#### expect
some 0
#### case 22 liveCells
effect Ask { ask() -> Int }  fn work() -> Int / {Ask} { let mut x = 7; x + ask() }
    fn main() -> Int { handle work() { ask(k) => cancel(k); 3 } }
#### expect
some 0
#### case 23 liveCells
#### An arm that does not use its resumption cancels it, which releases what the
#### suspended computation held.
effect Ask { ask() -> Int }  fn work() -> Int / {Ask} { let mut x = 7; x + ask() }
    fn main() -> Int { handle work() { ask(k) => 3 } }
#### expect
some 0
#### case 24 liveCells
#### A resumption passed to something that forgets it keeps what it captured: a
#### known leak.
effect Ask { ask() -> Int }  fn work() -> Int / {Ask} { let mut x = 7; x + ask() }
    fn forget<T>(k: T) -> Int { 3 }
    fn main() -> Int { handle work() { ask(k) => forget(k) } }
#### expect
some 1
#### case 25 cleanups
#### Cleanups: installed, started, and left in a resumption that was never taken.
#### The first is always the sum of the other two (`Core.cleanup_accounting`).
effect Ask { ask() }  fn work() -> Int / {Ask} { defer print(1); ask(); 0 }
    fn main() -> Int { handle work() { ask(k) => k(()) } }
#### expect
some (1, 1, 0)
#### case 26 cleanups
effect Ask { ask() }  fn work() -> Int / {Ask} { defer print(1); ask(); 0 }
    fn main() -> Int { handle work() { ask(k) => 7 } }
#### expect
some (1, 1, 0)
#### case 27 cleanups
#### (A resumption a function lets go is given up there, by a cleanup of the
#### function's own: its two cleanups start, and that one.)
effect Ask { ask() }  fn work() -> Int / {Ask} { defer print(1); defer print(2); ask(); 0 }
    fn forget<T>(k: T) -> Int { 7 }
    fn main() -> Int { handle work() { ask(k) => forget(k) } }
#### expect
some (3, 3, 0)
#### case 28 cleanups
effect Fail { fail() -> ! }  fn work() -> Int / {Fail} { defer print(1); fail(); 0 }
    fn main() -> Int { handle work() { fail() => 7 } }
#### expect
some (1, 1, 0)
#### case 29 diagnose
#### Without the checker, a callable that outlives its variable finds the cell gone.
fn mk() -> fn() -> Int { let mut n = 0; { n = n + 1; n } }  fn main() -> Int { mk()() }
#### expect
a reference is used after the cell it points to was freed
#### case 30 diagnose
#### The checker does not let a cleanup perform effects. The machines still give
#### such a program a meaning: the cleanup runs in place, inside the handlers it
#### was registered inside, and an arm that resumes it never sees its result.
effect Tick { tick() }  effect Stop { stop(Int) -> ! }
    fn work() -> Int / {Tick, Stop} { defer tick(); stop(1) }
    fn main() -> Int { handle (handle work() { tick(k) => k(()) + 1 }) { stop(v) => v } }
#### expect
did not go wrong
#### case 31 diagnose
effect Stop { stop(Int) -> ! }
    fn work() -> Int / {Stop} { defer stop(2); stop(1) }
    fn main() -> Int { handle work() { stop(v) => v } }
#### expect
did not go wrong
